3. Are not currently barred from contribution or discussion regarding this repository.
## Etiquette
### Conducting Yourself
Please be a civil person, and try not to stir trouble.  
This repository is a place for code and collaboration, 
and not for spam, politics, harassment, insults, threats or anything overtly NSFW.

Please also be receptive to feedback and participate in discussion.

If you are making people uncomfortable or behaving unacceptably, 
you may be barred from contributing or participating in any discussion, through any medium, at our discretion.

This does not restrict the right to access the source-code afforded to you by the AGPL licence.
### Using Git
#### Commit Messages
All commit messages **must** be written using [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/en/v1.0.0/).  
If they do not, they will likely be rebased and renamed before merging, or outright rejected.

If you use a large language model, please include an `Assisted-by: <tool>:<model>` tag in the footer.
Some historical commits are missing these, but we now mandate such a disclosure.

#### Branching
The 'trunk' branch of this repository is called `main`. This branch has protection rules, 
and should never be pushed to directly unless merging from elsewhere.  
We do not distinguish a `development` or `staging` branch from a `stable` branch at this stage.

In most cases, you can simply fork this repository and merge from a branch named however you would like.  
If you are submitting a branch to the main repository, you must comply with the following conventions.

Branch names should have a one-or-two word description of the target feature or fix.  
They should not be prefixed with `fix`, `patch` or similar.  

Branches can be 'foldered' if appropriate, 
and folder names should also be one-or-two word summaries. (eg. `integration/ocr`)  
If you are unsure how to name a branch, create an issue and name the branch `issues/#<issue number>`.  

Obviously, Git must also support the name of your branch. 
You can read more about the technical requirements in `man git-check-ref-format`.

#### Issues
Issues should be used to report bugs, problems and to request features, 
when you are not intending to implement said feature yourself.  
Where applicable, they should be written and managed the same way as Pull Requests.

#### Pull Requests
Pull requests should be titled using a one-sentence description of the changes.

A Pull Request should be an isolated set of changes and target the branch they are based upon. 
Changes from one branch should not "piggy-back" on another in order to end-up in `main`.[^1]

If a PR is blocked by any others, as well as being based onto the other branch, 
it should reference that branch by number[^2].
If a PR is a draft and has things still to complete, 
the description should contain a checklist of what is still outstanding.

Once a Pull Request is complete (not blocked and not marked as a draft), a member of the original team, 
or another appropriate stakeholder should review it.   
This involves looking over the code using the ‘Submit Review’ button on the ‘Files Changed’ page and 
providing some criticism, the author of the PR should then go back and address said criticism.  
Review should be a back and forth, rather than just one monolithic list of complaints.

Pull Requests are also automatically checked using `./gradlew check` via. GitHub Actions, to ensure that all tests pass, 
and that the code meets our style guidelines.

Additions of dependencies and changes to existing tests should be given particular scrutiny, and if possible, 
should be reviewed by a second person. For this reason, changes of this form are strongly discouraged,
unless the expected behaviour has changed as a result of your changes.  
On the other hand, the introduction of new tests is important to ensure the behaviour of any new features, 
and  

Once the reviewer is happy and the automated tests pass, they can mark the PR as approved, and merge it.

## Quality
### AI Generation
> TLDR; While we do not outright ban AI contributions, please [disclose the assistance](#commit-messages),
understand the implications of using such tools, and give your work some thought.


Please be aware that we are likely to be picky with what AI-generated code is included in our repository, 
and will not accept any undisclosed AI-generated or -assisted contributions.
You can disclose the AI contribution with a footer as described in [Commit Messages](#commit-messages)

AI generated code may be the source of quality concerns, and may also lead to legal concerns in the future.  
Please remember that you do not own the copyright to any AI-generated code, and you may be plagiarising from a source
with a licence incompatible with the AGPL licence used by this software.   
If any disputes of this nature arise, you may be deemed responsible for introducing plagiarised code to the codebase.   
O'Reilly Media published [an excellent article](https://www.oreilly.com/radar/who-owns-the-code-claude-wrote/)
about the state of affairs regarding AI and Code Copyright back in June, if you would like to read more.

### Code
Documentation is optional, but much appreciated.  
Avoid comments that re-iterate the code directly.
If a statement is complex enough to warrant a comment describing it, 
it may be worth trying to encapsulate it in a function.

Core functionality of code should be tested alongside any edge-cases.
During our initial development, we were modelled by _Agile_, and most of our tests target a specific _User Story_.  
This is not a requirement anymore.

The code is heavily modularised, and code should go in whatever folder is most appropriate.
Breaking changes should be avoided as best as possible and may be best suited 
as a seperate implementation in some way or another.

### Dependencies
Introducing a new dependency should be a last-resort, and its version should be locked as best as possible.  
Dependencies should be listed in the version catalogue rather than an individual build file.  
`buildSrc` should manage any heavy configuration, rather than an individual `build.gradle.kts`.

Currently, this project only targets Kotlin-JVM, and so Java dependencies are fine.
## Style
### General
Where possible, please use Australian English (Australian English sits between British and American English).  
This means using '_ou_' in words like _colour_, using the _s_ in words like _backwards_, 
and using _s_ in words like _finalise_.  
Australian English typically leans towards British English, but uses certain elements of American English, such as
the nouns _eggplant_ or _soccer_. 
Australian English also has some of its own idiosyncrasies such as the words _capsicum_ or _footpath_. 

If you are unsure about a word, try to support multiple spellings[^3], 
otherwise, a reviewer may change some spellings and phrasings before merging.

This is not a hard-and-fast rule, and any dialect should be acceptable for "meta"-writing, 
such as discussing Pull Requests and Issues, or when quoting prior work.
### Code
#### Formatting
All code should follow our code-style conventions.  
Running `./gradlew fmt` and autoformatting all source code will be sufficient. 

- Kotlin code should be formatted with KtFmt
- Python code should be formatted with Black and comply with PEP-8.

Furthermore, even though it is not required, code should aim to be clear and readable, with consistent whitespace.  
Particularly hard-to-read code may be edited or rejected even when an auto-formatter approves it. 

#### Naming Conventions
All code should follow the following naming conventions:

| Element           | Naming Case             |
|-------------------|-------------------------|
| Package           | `lowercase`             |
| Class / Interface | `PascalCase`            |
| Variable          | `camelCase`             |
| Enum Value        | `PascalCase`            |
| Function          | `camelCase`             |
| Constant          | `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE`      |
| Test Function     | `lowercase with spaces` |

##### Unused Variables
Unused variables should be prefixed with `_`.

##### Naming a Test Function
The test function naming can be done using backticks in Kotlin:
```kotlin
fun `test name goes here`() {}
```
In Python, if we use it in production, we intend to run tests as a `PythonScript`, 
and read the tests as the members of a dict:
```python
def __test_actual_name():
    pass

{"test name goes here": __test_actual_name}
```
### Documentation
#### CLI
- Subcommands should be verbs in simple future tense.  
_They should make sense in a sentence like "\<command name\> will \<help message first line>"._  
 Any following lines should be notes or clarifications and use the command name as an implied subject.

- Options are either a '_flag_' or 'value':  
*Flag*s are boolean or counted options, with no additional information.  
*Value*s are options that take additional information as the next argument.
    - _Flag_ help messages be in the same form as a subcommand.
    - _Value_ help messages should be a noun with an optional "that" conjunction.  
  _They should make sense in a sentence like "\<command name> is (a/the) \<help message>"._  
  There should not be any following lines.
#### In-code
Documentation comments are styled per-language.
- Kotlin Documentation should be in [KDoc](https://kotlinlang.org/docs/kotlin-doc.html).
- Python Documentation should be in [PyDoc](https://docs.python.org/3/library/pydoc.html).

We currently have no automatic generator for the documentation, however this may change in the future.
#### Other
There are no strict requirements for documentation outside of the code,
however, all new documentation should be written in GitHub Flavoured Markdown.

A number of documents are exported from the Confluence space we used during initial development.
If you are looking to change or add to one of these documents, please convert it to Markdown.  
If you want to partially automate this, 
you can use a tool like [pandoc](https://pandoc.org/) to convert the exported HTML directly into Markdown.
Do note that this will usually need some additional editing in order for it to be of an acceptable quality.

When documentation is meant to face the user, such as `README.md`, 
anything inside [`docs/`](docs), that isn't marked as internal, or as program output, 
please ensure that it doesn't assume internal knowledge of the tooling.  
For example, until recently, some of the CLI documentation outwardly described its behaviour 
in terms of the internal `Switchboard` framework. This information is not useful to an end user, 
and should not be in such documentation.


[^1]: "Piggy-backing" is when a set of changes is carried onto a branch via another.
        For example, if branch `A` implements some feature, and branch `B` requires that feature;
        `B` should be based on `A` and should have its Pull Request target `A`. 
        Even if `B` is approved, it should only be merged into `A`, and not `main` until `A` is also approved.
[^2]: GitHub uses the format `#<number>` to identify these, which should auto-complete while in GitHub.
[^3]: For example, the CLI supports `finalise` and `finalize`.